NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"temporal-group-key-oracle-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Temporal group-key oracle review"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reviewed-state-and-contract","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed state and contract"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Base: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"33a194941c8d51f8f98babb999fef2987dd6ff8b"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"origin/main"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed executable head: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"6d3e58ec"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Owner: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db-ivm/tests/temporal-group-key-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The user confirmed that db-ivm "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":" must keep different Temporal values in\ndifferent groups. This repair uses db-ivm's established hash key domain: a\nTemporal kind and its string representation identify a key. The oracle checks\nthe public grouped result after one input batch. It covers the eight Temporal\nkinds recognized by hashing, with two different values and matching fresh\nvalues for each kind. The model counts those descriptors in a plain Map."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The original serializer treated each Temporal value as an empty plain object.\nOn the base commit, a public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":" of January 15 and June 15 PlainDates\nemitted one group with count 2. The new oracle failed at that public checkpoint\nfor all eight kinds: it observed one count-2 group where it expected two\ncount-1 groups. Eight matching-value controls passed. A direct serialization\ncontrol also failed because a Temporal value and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{}"},{"type":"text","value":" both encoded as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{}"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fix shares the existing Temporal discriminator with the serializer. The\nserializer uses a tagged kind-and-value tuple for Temporal values at any\nsupported structural depth. The existing hash rule is unchanged. The repair\nadds seven net production source lines; tests and contract records are separate."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"oracle-requirements","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Oracle requirements"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001: authority and limits"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The explicit user decision permits Temporal group keys. Existing db-ivm hashing defines the bounded kind-and-string domain. The oracle and coverage map name the one-batch checkpoint and exclusions."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002: independent judgment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass within that domain. A plain Map counts fixture values by Temporal kind and string. The model calls no db-ivm hash, serializer, or operator. The established hash domain is the separately justified semantic base; native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".equals()"},{"type":"text","value":" is a distinct unresolved policy."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003: visible responsibilities"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The opening states the law and limits. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"temporalCases"},{"type":"text","value":" is the bounded grammar, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedGroups"},{"type":"text","value":" is the model, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"observedGroups"},{"type":"text","value":" drives public D2 "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":", and the tests compare after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"graph.run()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004: generated grammar controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The oracle enumerates a fixed matrix; it does not claim generated-history coverage. Every listed kind runs distinct and matching cases."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005: path and observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The driver imports the package entry point and observes every group output. It compares group identity and count. Its recorder keeps each emitted row; it cannot hide the one-group collision by overwriting a key in a Map."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006: checker calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The unchanged serializer was the plausible wrong design. It reached public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":" and failed eight value assertions, not setup or timeout. The object control failed separately."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007: campaigns and replay"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. No important generated property is added. The fixed cases are individually named and directly runnable."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008: stateful-model minimality"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. Expected groups come from stateless recomputation of one batch."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009: vocabulary mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. A fixture value is a db-ivm relation key inside a record. The model's descriptor combines the established Temporal kind and string identity; it is not a production hash or serialized key."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010: failure and cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The in-memory graph has no acquired external resource or shrinker. The failing assertion preserves the observed group and checkpoint."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011: independent second formulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The review identified native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Temporal.equals()"},{"type":"text","value":" as a plausible different equality policy. No db-ivm contract selects it. The coverage map retains this exact decision; an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".equals()"},{"type":"text","value":" oracle would impose new behavior outside this repair."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record supplies ORC-012 evidence for executable head "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"6d3e58ec"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"verification-and-remaining-scope","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verification and remaining scope"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The focused oracle changed from 9 failed and 8 passed tests on the base commit\nto 17 passed on the repaired head. The full db-ivm suite passed 576 tests in\n40 files. Package TypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, Vite build, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"git diff --check"},{"type":"text","value":" passed. Vitest ran with at most two threads."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The oracle does not cover incremental retractions. Symbols and cyclic keys\nremain unsupported by the serializer. Mutable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RegExp.lastIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" does not\nbelong to this Temporal law. An independent reviewer reproduced another\nboundary: two ZonedDateTimes with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[UTC]"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[Etc/UTC]"},{"type":"text","value":" satisfy native\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".equals()"},{"type":"text","value":" but have different kind-and-string keys. The coverage map records\nthe native-equality decision. This PR does not change that hash behavior."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"external-review-follow-up-on-f25fff0d3eeb655075ecc9743d693c541f7bf92d","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External-review follow-up on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"f25fff0d3eeb655075ecc9743d693c541f7bf92d"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The review of PR #1919 found that the first oracle model classified inputs with\nthe same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Symbol.toStringTag"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toString()"},{"type":"text","value":" calls as production. This could\nhide a collision between a real Temporal value and a plain object with a\nspoofed tag. On starting head "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c08a3201b3cf166ac25494302ea92ea1d8b2bcb6"},{"type":"text","value":",\na plain object with an own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Temporal.PlainDate"},{"type":"text","value":" tag, a non-enumerable date-like\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toString()"},{"type":"text","value":", and an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extra"},{"type":"text","value":" property entered the Temporal branch. Public\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":" emitted one count-2 group for that object and a genuine PlainDate.\nThe new oracle case failed at the group-count assertion: expected two groups,\nobserved one. The original model would have expected that one group too."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The follow-up model counts fixture-assigned identity classes. It no longer calls\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Symbol.toStringTag"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toString()"},{"type":"text","value":" to derive expected groups. The detector now\nrequires the accepted tag to exist on the input's prototype. The same oracle\ncase passes, and a second case proves that a genuine Temporal value with its\nown matching tag still joins its matching group. This check prevents the\noriginal plain-object collision without treating a matching own tag as proof of\nTemporal identity. The entire db-ivm suite passed: 578 tests in 40 files.\nTypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, Vite build, and diff checks passed."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Follow-up outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The law remains two distinct group-key identities and one matching identity after one batch. The established kind-and-string domain stays the authority."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixture labels define expected classes. The model does not use the production Temporal classifier."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The opening contract, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"temporalCases"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedGroupCounts"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"observedGroupCounts"},{"type":"text","value":", and assertions expose the five oracle responsibilities."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable: the finite Temporal-kind matrix is not a generated property."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":" output supplies group counts after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"graph.run()"},{"type":"text","value":". The recorder also checks positive weights and unique emitted keys."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The original detector survived the old classifier but failed the new spoofed-object case at the public group-count checkpoint. The repaired detector passed the same case."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable: no generated property or replay campaign changed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable: the reference counts one stateless input batch."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixture labels represent expected group-key identity classes. They do not represent a production hash or serialized key."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable: the in-memory graph has no acquired resource or shrinking path."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fixture-label formulation is independent of the kind-and-string classifier. It rejects the named shared fault."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This addendum records each applicable requirement for the exact follow-up head."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The current detector still accepts a custom prototype that advertises a\nTemporal tag and supplies a matching "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toString()"},{"type":"text","value":" method. A direct probe found\nthat such an object still serializes to the same key as a genuine PlainDate.\nThe coverage map owns that remaining counterfeit-prototype witness and the\nnative/polyfill brand decision. No claim of complete spoofing resistance follows\nfrom this change. Also, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Temporal.Duration.compare(PT1H, PT60M)"},{"type":"text","value":" returns zero,\nbut "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":" emits two count-1 groups under the established string-key domain.\nThe coverage map retains that equality-policy decision with the ZonedDateTime\nalias example."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"second-review-on-b9b59c089dd574b5eed7672a66b6768808837919","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Second review on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b9b59c089dd574b5eed7672a66b6768808837919"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The second review challenged the remaining custom-prototype case named in the\ncoverage map. A plain object can inherit a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Temporal.PlainDate"},{"type":"text","value":" tag and a\nmatching "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toString()"},{"type":"text","value":" from a custom prototype. With an own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extra: 1"},{"type":"text","value":" field,\nthis object is a supported structural value distinct from a genuine PlainDate.\nThe current public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":" merged those two rows. A new primary-oracle case\nfailed at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"graph.run()"},{"type":"text","value":": expected group counts "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[1, 1]"},{"type":"text","value":", observed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[2]"},{"type":"text","value":".\nThis is an in-scope counterexample to the plain-object versus Temporal group-key\nidentity law. It keeps the broader tag-spoofing class open. The RED case remains\nlocal and uncommitted while the compatibility policy is undecided; the PR must\nnot gain an active failing test without its repair."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A trusted brand method can distinguish this counterfeit. The local\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"temporal-polyfill"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PlainDate.prototype.toString.call(value)"},{"type":"text","value":" rejects it.\nNode 24's native Temporal method also rejects it. Under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--harmony-temporal"},{"type":"text","value":",\nthe native method accepts genuine values from a separate VM realm for all eight\nrecognized Temporal kinds. The local polyfill method rejects a genuine\nPlainDate from a separately loaded polyfill copy with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TypeError: Invalid calling context"},{"type":"text","value":". Current "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts that cross-copy value and merges\nit with a same-value local polyfill PlainDate into one count-2 group. Thus a\nlocal-polyfill-only brand gate would break an observed public behavior."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The smallest policy-dependent options are:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Trust the runtime's native Temporal constructors and one imported\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"temporal-polyfill"},{"type":"text","value":" copy. Call each trusted prototype method on the input.\nThis preserves native cross-realm and local-polyfill values. It cannot\nauthenticate a separately loaded foreign polyfill copy. Treating that copy\nas an ordinary object could merge distinct foreign dates with no enumerable\nfields, so this option needs a declared unsupported boundary or an error.\nIt also needs a runtime polyfill dependency and roughly 15–25 additional\nproduction lines before simplification. The final count needs the diff."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add a trusted registration path for other polyfill copies. A registered\nconstructor supplies its brand-checking prototype method. This can preserve\ncross-copy grouping after registration, but requires a new public capability\nand an explicit decision about unregistered values. The extra registry,\nlifecycle, and API need more production code than option 1."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fail immediately for untrusted Temporal-like inputs. This prevents a silent\ncollision, but it rejects valid plain objects that advertise a Temporal tag\nand does not preserve existing cross-copy grouping."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reading a constructor or method from the candidate object is not a sound brand\ncheck: a counterfeit can supply both. JavaScript exposes no generic Temporal\ninternal-slot check that accepts arbitrary independent polyfill copies without\na trusted intrinsic or registration. The user is deciding whether separately\nloaded polyfill copies must remain supported. Until that decision, this review\nitem is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"confirmed-open"},{"type":"text","value":"; no production change, changeset, PR-body update, or\npush follows from this review. Production-code weight for this second review is\nzero. The Duration alias-equivalence decision remains separate and unchanged."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewer assessment for this second finding: technically accurate and high\nsignal. It reached the public boundary and named a residual case that the\ncoverage map already owned. The request for a sound cross-realm brand check\nexposes a real compatibility choice, so the proposed fix is incomplete until\nthat choice is made. This review sample supports a positive reviewer\nrecommendation, with the policy dependency stated explicitly."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"full-rereview-own-only-temporal-tag-on-b9b59c089dd574b5eed7672a66b6768808837919","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full rereview: own-only Temporal tag on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b9b59c089dd574b5eed7672a66b6768808837919"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R19-4 proposed that a genuine Temporal value with only an own\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Symbol.toStringTag"},{"type":"text","value":" would fail the current prototype-tag gate. This state does\nnot arise from ordinary native or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"temporal-polyfill"},{"type":"text","value":" construction. It is still\nreachable through legal JavaScript operations. For both implementations, the\nprobe created two equal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PlainDate"},{"type":"text","value":" values, added an own matching tag to one,\nthen moved that value to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Object.prototype"},{"type":"text","value":". It installed the implementation's\nintrinsic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toString"},{"type":"text","value":" method as a non-enumerable own property. The intrinsic\nstill returned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2024-01-15"},{"type":"text","value":" for the changed value, proving that its Temporal\nbrand survived the prototype change."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An own matching tag with the original prototype remained recognized by\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isTemporal"},{"type":"text","value":". With the changed prototype, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isTemporal"},{"type":"text","value":" returned false and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"serializeValue"},{"type":"text","value":" produced "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{}"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of the genuine date's Temporal key.\nPublic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":" emitted two count-1 groups for the equal dates, while the\nfixture-defined Temporal identity law expects one count-2 group. The same\nresult occurred with the local polyfill and Node 24 native Temporal under\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--harmony-temporal"},{"type":"text","value":". The claim is technically true for a valid, uncommon\noperation. The current contract says Temporal values are valid group keys and\ndoes not exclude prototype changes. The broader trusted-brand decision from\nthe second review also owns this case. No stricter gate or RED test is pushed\nwhile the user decides the supported cross-copy polyfill domain."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The rereview also called "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PT1H"},{"type":"text","value":" versus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PT60M"},{"type":"text","value":" separation \"by-design.\" That\nphrase describes the existing kind-and-string key rule, but it overstates the\nalias policy. The coverage map explicitly says whether equal-comparing\nDurations should coalesce needs a separate contract decision. The first review\nrecord makes the same distinction. Therefore the current behavior is documented,\nbut the alias-equivalence policy is open. This review does not change it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full-rereview ledger: two source claims. R19-4 is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"confirmed-open"},{"type":"text","value":" with the\npublic groupBy probe above. The Duration wording is a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"design-decision"},{"type":"text","value":" with\nits destination in the Temporal group-key coverage map. Neither item caused a\nproduction edit, test commit, PR-body change, or push. Production-code weight\nfor this rereview is zero. Reviewer assessment: R19-4 identified a real but\nuncommon legal state, while the \"by-design\" summary was too strong. The\ntechnical signal is good; the review should keep documented behavior separate\nfrom an approved product decision."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"product-scope-decision","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Product scope decision"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The maintainer chose ordinary native Temporal values or one polyfill copy as\nthe supported group-key inputs. Code that replaces a Temporal prototype or\ncreates a custom prototype that impersonates Temporal is outside this contract.\nSeparately loaded polyfill copies are also outside it. The RED probes above\nremain evidence of those behaviors; they do not establish an in-scope product\nbug under the chosen domain. The active oracle keeps its ordinary own-tag\nplain-object control and does not include a failing altered-prototype case."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The maintainer also chose to keep alternate representations separate.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PT1H"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PT60M"},{"type":"text","value":" therefore remain separate groups even though\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Temporal.Duration.compare()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns zero. This follows the established\nkind-and-string key rule. The coverage map and oracle opening now state both\ndecisions. No production code or new runtime dependency is needed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The updated oracle checks a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PT1H"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PT60M"},{"type":"text","value":" pair at the public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":"\ncheckpoint. The 20 Temporal oracle tests and db-ivm TypeScript check pass."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"temporal-group-key-oracle-review","text":"Temporal group-key oracle review","level":1},{"id":"reviewed-state-and-contract","text":"Reviewed state and contract","level":2},{"id":"oracle-requirements","text":"Oracle requirements","level":2},{"id":"verification-and-remaining-scope","text":"Verification and remaining scope","level":2},{"id":"external-review-follow-up-on-f25fff0d3eeb655075ecc9743d693c541f7bf92d","text":"External-review follow-up on f25fff0d3eeb655075ecc9743d693c541f7bf92d","level":2},{"id":"second-review-on-b9b59c089dd574b5eed7672a66b6768808837919","text":"Second review on b9b59c089dd574b5eed7672a66b6768808837919","level":2},{"id":"full-rereview-own-only-temporal-tag-on-b9b59c089dd574b5eed7672a66b6768808837919","text":"Full rereview: own-only Temporal tag on b9b59c089dd574b5eed7672a66b6768808837919","level":2},{"id":"product-scope-decision","text":"Product scope decision","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Temporal group-key oracle review\n\n## Reviewed state and contract\n\n- Base: `33a194941c8d51f8f98babb999fef2987dd6ff8b` (`origin/main`).\n- Reviewed executable head: `6d3e58ec`.\n- Owner: `packages/db-ivm/tests/temporal-group-key-oracle.test.ts`.\n\nThe user confirmed that db-ivm `groupBy` must keep different Temporal values in\ndifferent groups. This repair uses db-ivm's established hash key domain: a\nTemporal kind and its string representation identify a key. The oracle checks\nthe public grouped result after one input batch. It covers the eight Temporal\nkinds recognized by hashing, with two different values and matching fresh\nvalues for each kind. The model counts those descriptors in a plain Map.\n\nThe original serializer treated each Temporal value as an empty plain object.\nOn the base commit, a public `groupBy` of January 15 and June 15 PlainDates\nemitted one group with count 2. The new oracle failed at that public checkpoint\nfor all eight kinds: it observed one count-2 group where it expected two\ncount-1 groups. Eight matching-value controls passed. A direct serialization\ncontrol also failed because a Temporal value and `{}` both encoded as `{}`.\n\nThe fix shares the existing Temporal discriminator with the serializer. The\nserializer uses a tagged kind-and-value tuple for Temporal values at any\nsupported structural depth. The existing hash rule is unchanged. The repair\nadds seven net production source lines; tests and contract records are separate.\n\n## Oracle requirements\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001: authority and limits | Pass. The explicit user decision permits Temporal group keys. Existing db-ivm hashing defines the bounded kind-and-string domain. The oracle and coverage map name the one-batch checkpoint and exclusions. |\n| ORC-002: independent judgment | Pass within that domain. A plain Map counts fixture values by Temporal kind and string. The model calls no db-ivm hash, serializer, or operator. The established hash domain is the separately justified semantic base; native `.equals()` is a distinct unresolved policy. |\n| ORC-003: visible responsibilities | Pass. The opening states the law and limits. `temporalCases` is the bounded grammar, `expectedGroups` is the model, `observedGroups` drives public D2 `groupBy`, and the tests compare after `graph.run()`. |\n| ORC-004: generated grammar controls | Not applicable. The oracle enumerates a fixed matrix; it does not claim generated-history coverage. Every listed kind runs distinct and matching cases. |\n| ORC-005: path and observation | Pass. The driver imports the package entry point and observes every group output. It compares group identity and count. Its recorder keeps each emitted row; it cannot hide the one-group collision by overwriting a key in a Map. |\n| ORC-006: checker calibration | Pass. The unchanged serializer was the plausible wrong design. It reached public `groupBy` and failed eight value assertions, not setup or timeout. The object control failed separately. |\n| ORC-007: campaigns and replay | Not applicable. No important generated property is added. The fixed cases are individually named and directly runnable. |\n| ORC-008: stateful-model minimality | Not applicable. Expected groups come from stateless recomputation of one batch. |\n| ORC-009: vocabulary mapping | Pass. A fixture value is a db-ivm relation key inside a record. The model's descriptor combines the established Temporal kind and string identity; it is not a production hash or serialized key. |\n| ORC-010: failure and cleanup | Not applicable. The in-memory graph has no acquired external resource or shrinker. The failing assertion preserves the observed group and checkpoint. |\n| ORC-011: independent second formulation | The review identified native `Temporal.equals()` as a plausible different equality policy. No db-ivm contract selects it. The coverage map retains this exact decision; an `.equals()` oracle would impose new behavior outside this repair. |\n\nThis record supplies ORC-012 evidence for executable head `6d3e58ec`.\n\n## Verification and remaining scope\n\nThe focused oracle changed from 9 failed and 8 passed tests on the base commit\nto 17 passed on the repaired head. The full db-ivm suite passed 576 tests in\n40 files. Package TypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, Vite build, and\n`git diff --check` passed. Vitest ran with at most two threads.\n\nThe oracle does not cover incremental retractions. Symbols and cyclic keys\nremain unsupported by the serializer. Mutable `RegExp.lastIndex` does not\nbelong to this Temporal law. An independent reviewer reproduced another\nboundary: two ZonedDateTimes with `[UTC]` and `[Etc/UTC]` satisfy native\n`.equals()` but have different kind-and-string keys. The coverage map records\nthe native-equality decision. This PR does not change that hash behavior.\n\n## External-review follow-up on `f25fff0d3eeb655075ecc9743d693c541f7bf92d`\n\nThe review of PR #1919 found that the first oracle model classified inputs with\nthe same `Symbol.toStringTag` and `toString()` calls as production. This could\nhide a collision between a real Temporal value and a plain object with a\nspoofed tag. On starting head `c08a3201b3cf166ac25494302ea92ea1d8b2bcb6`,\na plain object with an own `Temporal.PlainDate` tag, a non-enumerable date-like\n`toString()`, and an `extra` property entered the Temporal branch. Public\n`groupBy` emitted one count-2 group for that object and a genuine PlainDate.\nThe new oracle case failed at the group-count assertion: expected two groups,\nobserved one. The original model would have expected that one group too.\n\nThe follow-up model counts fixture-assigned identity classes. It no longer calls\n`Symbol.toStringTag` or `toString()` to derive expected groups. The detector now\nrequires the accepted tag to exist on the input's prototype. The same oracle\ncase passes, and a second case proves that a genuine Temporal value with its\nown matching tag still joins its matching group. This check prevents the\noriginal plain-object collision without treating a matching own tag as proof of\nTemporal identity. The entire db-ivm suite passed: 578 tests in 40 files.\nTypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, Vite build, and diff checks passed.\n\n| Requirement | Follow-up outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | The law remains two distinct group-key identities and one matching identity after one batch. The established kind-and-string domain stays the authority. |\n| ORC-002 | Fixture labels define expected classes. The model does not use the production Temporal classifier. |\n| ORC-003 | The opening contract, `temporalCases`, `expectedGroupCounts`, `observedGroupCounts`, and assertions expose the five oracle responsibilities. |\n| ORC-004 | Not applicable: the finite Temporal-kind matrix is not a generated property. |\n| ORC-005 | The public `groupBy` output supplies group counts after `graph.run()`. The recorder also checks positive weights and unique emitted keys. |\n| ORC-006 | The original detector survived the old classifier but failed the new spoofed-object case at the public group-count checkpoint. The repaired detector passed the same case. |\n| ORC-007 | Not applicable: no generated property or replay campaign changed. |\n| ORC-008 | Not applicable: the reference counts one stateless input batch. |\n| ORC-009 | Fixture labels represent expected group-key identity classes. They do not represent a production hash or serialized key. |\n| ORC-010 | Not applicable: the in-memory graph has no acquired resource or shrinking path. |\n| ORC-011 | The fixture-label formulation is independent of the kind-and-string classifier. It rejects the named shared fault. |\n| ORC-012 | This addendum records each applicable requirement for the exact follow-up head. |\n\nThe current detector still accepts a custom prototype that advertises a\nTemporal tag and supplies a matching `toString()` method. A direct probe found\nthat such an object still serializes to the same key as a genuine PlainDate.\nThe coverage map owns that remaining counterfeit-prototype witness and the\nnative/polyfill brand decision. No claim of complete spoofing resistance follows\nfrom this change. Also, `Temporal.Duration.compare(PT1H, PT60M)` returns zero,\nbut `groupBy` emits two count-1 groups under the established string-key domain.\nThe coverage map retains that equality-policy decision with the ZonedDateTime\nalias example.\n\n## Second review on `b9b59c089dd574b5eed7672a66b6768808837919`\n\nThe second review challenged the remaining custom-prototype case named in the\ncoverage map. A plain object can inherit a `Temporal.PlainDate` tag and a\nmatching `toString()` from a custom prototype. With an own `extra: 1` field,\nthis object is a supported structural value distinct from a genuine PlainDate.\nThe current public `groupBy` merged those two rows. A new primary-oracle case\nfailed at `graph.run()`: expected group counts `[1, 1]`, observed `[2]`.\nThis is an in-scope counterexample to the plain-object versus Temporal group-key\nidentity law. It keeps the broader tag-spoofing class open. The RED case remains\nlocal and uncommitted while the compatibility policy is undecided; the PR must\nnot gain an active failing test without its repair.\n\nA trusted brand method can distinguish this counterfeit. The local\n`temporal-polyfill` `PlainDate.prototype.toString.call(value)` rejects it.\nNode 24's native Temporal method also rejects it. Under `--harmony-temporal`,\nthe native method accepts genuine values from a separate VM realm for all eight\nrecognized Temporal kinds. The local polyfill method rejects a genuine\nPlainDate from a separately loaded polyfill copy with `TypeError: Invalid\ncalling context`. Current `groupBy` accepts that cross-copy value and merges\nit with a same-value local polyfill PlainDate into one count-2 group. Thus a\nlocal-polyfill-only brand gate would break an observed public behavior.\n\nThe smallest policy-dependent options are:\n\n1. Trust the runtime's native Temporal constructors and one imported\n   `temporal-polyfill` copy. Call each trusted prototype method on the input.\n   This preserves native cross-realm and local-polyfill values. It cannot\n   authenticate a separately loaded foreign polyfill copy. Treating that copy\n   as an ordinary object could merge distinct foreign dates with no enumerable\n   fields, so this option needs a declared unsupported boundary or an error.\n   It also needs a runtime polyfill dependency and roughly 15–25 additional\n   production lines before simplification. The final count needs the diff.\n2. Add a trusted registration path for other polyfill copies. A registered\n   constructor supplies its brand-checking prototype method. This can preserve\n   cross-copy grouping after registration, but requires a new public capability\n   and an explicit decision about unregistered values. The extra registry,\n   lifecycle, and API need more production code than option 1.\n3. Fail immediately for untrusted Temporal-like inputs. This prevents a silent\n   collision, but it rejects valid plain objects that advertise a Temporal tag\n   and does not preserve existing cross-copy grouping.\n\nReading a constructor or method from the candidate object is not a sound brand\ncheck: a counterfeit can supply both. JavaScript exposes no generic Temporal\ninternal-slot check that accepts arbitrary independent polyfill copies without\na trusted intrinsic or registration. The user is deciding whether separately\nloaded polyfill copies must remain supported. Until that decision, this review\nitem is `confirmed-open`; no production change, changeset, PR-body update, or\npush follows from this review. Production-code weight for this second review is\nzero. The Duration alias-equivalence decision remains separate and unchanged.\n\nReviewer assessment for this second finding: technically accurate and high\nsignal. It reached the public boundary and named a residual case that the\ncoverage map already owned. The request for a sound cross-realm brand check\nexposes a real compatibility choice, so the proposed fix is incomplete until\nthat choice is made. This review sample supports a positive reviewer\nrecommendation, with the policy dependency stated explicitly.\n\n## Full rereview: own-only Temporal tag on `b9b59c089dd574b5eed7672a66b6768808837919`\n\nR19-4 proposed that a genuine Temporal value with only an own\n`Symbol.toStringTag` would fail the current prototype-tag gate. This state does\nnot arise from ordinary native or `temporal-polyfill` construction. It is still\nreachable through legal JavaScript operations. For both implementations, the\nprobe created two equal `PlainDate` values, added an own matching tag to one,\nthen moved that value to `Object.prototype`. It installed the implementation's\nintrinsic `toString` method as a non-enumerable own property. The intrinsic\nstill returned `2024-01-15` for the changed value, proving that its Temporal\nbrand survived the prototype change.\n\nAn own matching tag with the original prototype remained recognized by\n`isTemporal`. With the changed prototype, `isTemporal` returned false and\n`serializeValue` produced `{}` instead of the genuine date's Temporal key.\nPublic `groupBy` emitted two count-1 groups for the equal dates, while the\nfixture-defined Temporal identity law expects one count-2 group. The same\nresult occurred with the local polyfill and Node 24 native Temporal under\n`--harmony-temporal`. The claim is technically true for a valid, uncommon\noperation. The current contract says Temporal values are valid group keys and\ndoes not exclude prototype changes. The broader trusted-brand decision from\nthe second review also owns this case. No stricter gate or RED test is pushed\nwhile the user decides the supported cross-copy polyfill domain.\n\nThe rereview also called `PT1H` versus `PT60M` separation \"by-design.\" That\nphrase describes the existing kind-and-string key rule, but it overstates the\nalias policy. The coverage map explicitly says whether equal-comparing\nDurations should coalesce needs a separate contract decision. The first review\nrecord makes the same distinction. Therefore the current behavior is documented,\nbut the alias-equivalence policy is open. This review does not change it.\n\nFull-rereview ledger: two source claims. R19-4 is `confirmed-open` with the\npublic groupBy probe above. The Duration wording is a `design-decision` with\nits destination in the Temporal group-key coverage map. Neither item caused a\nproduction edit, test commit, PR-body change, or push. Production-code weight\nfor this rereview is zero. Reviewer assessment: R19-4 identified a real but\nuncommon legal state, while the \"by-design\" summary was too strong. The\ntechnical signal is good; the review should keep documented behavior separate\nfrom an approved product decision.\n\n## Product scope decision\n\nThe maintainer chose ordinary native Temporal values or one polyfill copy as\nthe supported group-key inputs. Code that replaces a Temporal prototype or\ncreates a custom prototype that impersonates Temporal is outside this contract.\nSeparately loaded polyfill copies are also outside it. The RED probes above\nremain evidence of those behaviors; they do not establish an in-scope product\nbug under the chosen domain. The active oracle keeps its ordinary own-tag\nplain-object control and does not include a failing altered-prototype case.\n\nThe maintainer also chose to keep alternate representations separate.\n`PT1H` and `PT60M` therefore remain separate groups even though\n`Temporal.Duration.compare()` returns zero. This follows the established\nkind-and-string key rule. The coverage map and oracle opening now state both\ndecisions. No production code or new runtime dependency is needed.\n\nThe updated oracle checks a `PT1H`/`PT60M` pair at the public `groupBy`\ncheckpoint. The 20 Temporal oracle tests and db-ivm TypeScript check pass.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Temporal group-key oracle review\n\n## Reviewed state and contract\n\n- Base: `33a194941c8d51f8f98babb999fef2987dd6ff8b` (`origin/main`).\n- Reviewed executable head: `6d3e58ec`.\n- Owner: `packages/db-ivm/tests/temporal-group-key-oracle.test.ts`.\n\nThe user confirmed that db-ivm `groupBy` must keep different Temporal values in\ndifferent groups. This repair uses db-ivm's established hash key domain: a\nTemporal kind and its string representation identify a key. The oracle checks\nthe public grouped result after one input batch. It covers the eight Temporal\nkinds recognized by hashing, with two different values and matching fresh\nvalues for each kind. The model counts those descriptors in a plain Map.\n\nThe original serializer treated each Temporal value as an empty plain object.\nOn the base commit, a public `groupBy` of January 15 and June 15 PlainDates\nemitted one group with count 2. The new oracle failed at that public checkpoint\nfor all eight kinds: it observed one count-2 group where it expected two\ncount-1 groups. Eight matching-value controls passed. A direct serialization\ncontrol also failed because a Temporal value and `{}` both encoded as `{}`.\n\nThe fix shares the existing Temporal discriminator with the serializer. The\nserializer uses a tagged kind-and-value tuple for Temporal values at any\nsupported structural depth. The existing hash rule is unchanged. The repair\nadds seven net production source lines; tests and contract records are separate.\n\n## Oracle requirements\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001: authority and limits | Pass. The explicit user decision permits Temporal group keys. Existing db-ivm hashing defines the bounded kind-and-string domain. The oracle and coverage map name the one-batch checkpoint and exclusions. |\n| ORC-002: independent judgment | Pass within that domain. A plain Map counts fixture values by Temporal kind and string. The model calls no db-ivm hash, serializer, or operator. The established hash domain is the separately justified semantic base; native `.equals()` is a distinct unresolved policy. |\n| ORC-003: visible responsibilities | Pass. The opening states the law and limits. `temporalCases` is the bounded grammar, `expectedGroups` is the model, `observedGroups` drives public D2 `groupBy`, and the tests compare after `graph.run()`. |\n| ORC-004: generated grammar controls | Not applicable. The oracle enumerates a fixed matrix; it does not claim generated-history coverage. Every listed kind runs distinct and matching cases. |\n| ORC-005: path and observation | Pass. The driver imports the package entry point and observes every group output. It compares group identity and count. Its recorder keeps each emitted row; it cannot hide the one-group collision by overwriting a key in a Map. |\n| ORC-006: checker calibration | Pass. The unchanged serializer was the plausible wrong design. It reached public `groupBy` and failed eight value assertions, not setup or timeout. The object control failed separately. |\n| ORC-007: campaigns and replay | Not applicable. No important generated property is added. The fixed cases are individually named and directly runnable. |\n| ORC-008: stateful-model minimality | Not applicable. Expected groups come from stateless recomputation of one batch. |\n| ORC-009: vocabulary mapping | Pass. A fixture value is a db-ivm relation key inside a record. The model's descriptor combines the established Temporal kind and string identity; it is not a production hash or serialized key. |\n| ORC-010: failure and cleanup | Not applicable. The in-memory graph has no acquired external resource or shrinker. The failing assertion preserves the observed group and checkpoint. |\n| ORC-011: independent second formulation | The review identified native `Temporal.equals()` as a plausible different equality policy. No db-ivm contract selects it. The coverage map retains this exact decision; an `.equals()` oracle would impose new behavior outside this repair. |\n\nThis record supplies ORC-012 evidence for executable head `6d3e58ec`.\n\n## Verification and remaining scope\n\nThe focused oracle changed from 9 failed and 8 passed tests on the base commit\nto 17 passed on the repaired head. The full db-ivm suite passed 576 tests in\n40 files. Package TypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, Vite build, and\n`git diff --check` passed. Vitest ran with at most two threads.\n\nThe oracle does not cover incremental retractions. Symbols and cyclic keys\nremain unsupported by the serializer. Mutable `RegExp.lastIndex` does not\nbelong to this Temporal law. An independent reviewer reproduced another\nboundary: two ZonedDateTimes with `[UTC]` and `[Etc/UTC]` satisfy native\n`.equals()` but have different kind-and-string keys. The coverage map records\nthe native-equality decision. This PR does not change that hash behavior.\n\n## External-review follow-up on `f25fff0d3eeb655075ecc9743d693c541f7bf92d`\n\nThe review of PR #1919 found that the first oracle model classified inputs with\nthe same `Symbol.toStringTag` and `toString()` calls as production. This could\nhide a collision between a real Temporal value and a plain object with a\nspoofed tag. On starting head `c08a3201b3cf166ac25494302ea92ea1d8b2bcb6`,\na plain object with an own `Temporal.PlainDate` tag, a non-enumerable date-like\n`toString()`, and an `extra` property entered the Temporal branch. Public\n`groupBy` emitted one count-2 group for that object and a genuine PlainDate.\nThe new oracle case failed at the group-count assertion: expected two groups,\nobserved one. The original model would have expected that one group too.\n\nThe follow-up model counts fixture-assigned identity classes. It no longer calls\n`Symbol.toStringTag` or `toString()` to derive expected groups. The detector now\nrequires the accepted tag to exist on the input's prototype. The same oracle\ncase passes, and a second case proves that a genuine Temporal value with its\nown matching tag still joins its matching group. This check prevents the\noriginal plain-object collision without treating a matching own tag as proof of\nTemporal identity. The entire db-ivm suite passed: 578 tests in 40 files.\nTypeScript, changed-file ESLint, Prettier, Vite build, and diff checks passed.\n\n| Requirement | Follow-up outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | The law remains two distinct group-key identities and one matching identity after one batch. The established kind-and-string domain stays the authority. |\n| ORC-002 | Fixture labels define expected classes. The model does not use the production Temporal classifier. |\n| ORC-003 | The opening contract, `temporalCases`, `expectedGroupCounts`, `observedGroupCounts`, and assertions expose the five oracle responsibilities. |\n| ORC-004 | Not applicable: the finite Temporal-kind matrix is not a generated property. |\n| ORC-005 | The public `groupBy` output supplies group counts after `graph.run()`. The recorder also checks positive weights and unique emitted keys. |\n| ORC-006 | The original detector survived the old classifier but failed the new spoofed-object case at the public group-count checkpoint. The repaired detector passed the same case. |\n| ORC-007 | Not applicable: no generated property or replay campaign changed. |\n| ORC-008 | Not applicable: the reference counts one stateless input batch. |\n| ORC-009 | Fixture labels represent expected group-key identity classes. They do not represent a production hash or serialized key. |\n| ORC-010 | Not applicable: the in-memory graph has no acquired resource or shrinking path. |\n| ORC-011 | The fixture-label formulation is independent of the kind-and-string classifier. It rejects the named shared fault. |\n| ORC-012 | This addendum records each applicable requirement for the exact follow-up head. |\n\nThe current detector still accepts a custom prototype that advertises a\nTemporal tag and supplies a matching `toString()` method. A direct probe found\nthat such an object still serializes to the same key as a genuine PlainDate.\nThe coverage map owns that remaining counterfeit-prototype witness and the\nnative/polyfill brand decision. No claim of complete spoofing resistance follows\nfrom this change. Also, `Temporal.Duration.compare(PT1H, PT60M)` returns zero,\nbut `groupBy` emits two count-1 groups under the established string-key domain.\nThe coverage map retains that equality-policy decision with the ZonedDateTime\nalias example.\n\n## Second review on `b9b59c089dd574b5eed7672a66b6768808837919`\n\nThe second review challenged the remaining custom-prototype case named in the\ncoverage map. A plain object can inherit a `Temporal.PlainDate` tag and a\nmatching `toString()` from a custom prototype. With an own `extra: 1` field,\nthis object is a supported structural value distinct from a genuine PlainDate.\nThe current public `groupBy` merged those two rows. A new primary-oracle case\nfailed at `graph.run()`: expected group counts `[1, 1]`, observed `[2]`.\nThis is an in-scope counterexample to the plain-object versus Temporal group-key\nidentity law. It keeps the broader tag-spoofing class open. The RED case remains\nlocal and uncommitted while the compatibility policy is undecided; the PR must\nnot gain an active failing test without its repair.\n\nA trusted brand method can distinguish this counterfeit. The local\n`temporal-polyfill` `PlainDate.prototype.toString.call(value)` rejects it.\nNode 24's native Temporal method also rejects it. Under `--harmony-temporal`,\nthe native method accepts genuine values from a separate VM realm for all eight\nrecognized Temporal kinds. The local polyfill method rejects a genuine\nPlainDate from a separately loaded polyfill copy with `TypeError: Invalid\ncalling context`. Current `groupBy` accepts that cross-copy value and merges\nit with a same-value local polyfill PlainDate into one count-2 group. Thus a\nlocal-polyfill-only brand gate would break an observed public behavior.\n\nThe smallest policy-dependent options are:\n\n1. Trust the runtime's native Temporal constructors and one imported\n   `temporal-polyfill` copy. Call each trusted prototype method on the input.\n   This preserves native cross-realm and local-polyfill values. It cannot\n   authenticate a separately loaded foreign polyfill copy. Treating that copy\n   as an ordinary object could merge distinct foreign dates with no enumerable\n   fields, so this option needs a declared unsupported boundary or an error.\n   It also needs a runtime polyfill dependency and roughly 15–25 additional\n   production lines before simplification. The final count needs the diff.\n2. Add a trusted registration path for other polyfill copies. A registered\n   constructor supplies its brand-checking prototype method. This can preserve\n   cross-copy grouping after registration, but requires a new public capability\n   and an explicit decision about unregistered values. The extra registry,\n   lifecycle, and API need more production code than option 1.\n3. Fail immediately for untrusted Temporal-like inputs. This prevents a silent\n   collision, but it rejects valid plain objects that advertise a Temporal tag\n   and does not preserve existing cross-copy grouping.\n\nReading a constructor or method from the candidate object is not a sound brand\ncheck: a counterfeit can supply both. JavaScript exposes no generic Temporal\ninternal-slot check that accepts arbitrary independent polyfill copies without\na trusted intrinsic or registration. The user is deciding whether separately\nloaded polyfill copies must remain supported. Until that decision, this review\nitem is `confirmed-open`; no production change, changeset, PR-body update, or\npush follows from this review. Production-code weight for this second review is\nzero. The Duration alias-equivalence decision remains separate and unchanged.\n\nReviewer assessment for this second finding: technically accurate and high\nsignal. It reached the public boundary and named a residual case that the\ncoverage map already owned. The request for a sound cross-realm brand check\nexposes a real compatibility choice, so the proposed fix is incomplete until\nthat choice is made. This review sample supports a positive reviewer\nrecommendation, with the policy dependency stated explicitly.\n\n## Full rereview: own-only Temporal tag on `b9b59c089dd574b5eed7672a66b6768808837919`\n\nR19-4 proposed that a genuine Temporal value with only an own\n`Symbol.toStringTag` would fail the current prototype-tag gate. This state does\nnot arise from ordinary native or `temporal-polyfill` construction. It is still\nreachable through legal JavaScript operations. For both implementations, the\nprobe created two equal `PlainDate` values, added an own matching tag to one,\nthen moved that value to `Object.prototype`. It installed the implementation's\nintrinsic `toString` method as a non-enumerable own property. The intrinsic\nstill returned `2024-01-15` for the changed value, proving that its Temporal\nbrand survived the prototype change.\n\nAn own matching tag with the original prototype remained recognized by\n`isTemporal`. With the changed prototype, `isTemporal` returned false and\n`serializeValue` produced `{}` instead of the genuine date's Temporal key.\nPublic `groupBy` emitted two count-1 groups for the equal dates, while the\nfixture-defined Temporal identity law expects one count-2 group. The same\nresult occurred with the local polyfill and Node 24 native Temporal under\n`--harmony-temporal`. The claim is technically true for a valid, uncommon\noperation. The current contract says Temporal values are valid group keys and\ndoes not exclude prototype changes. The broader trusted-brand decision from\nthe second review also owns this case. No stricter gate or RED test is pushed\nwhile the user decides the supported cross-copy polyfill domain.\n\nThe rereview also called `PT1H` versus `PT60M` separation \"by-design.\" That\nphrase describes the existing kind-and-string key rule, but it overstates the\nalias policy. The coverage map explicitly says whether equal-comparing\nDurations should coalesce needs a separate contract decision. The first review\nrecord makes the same distinction. Therefore the current behavior is documented,\nbut the alias-equivalence policy is open. This review does not change it.\n\nFull-rereview ledger: two source claims. R19-4 is `confirmed-open` with the\npublic groupBy probe above. The Duration wording is a `design-decision` with\nits destination in the Temporal group-key coverage map. Neither item caused a\nproduction edit, test commit, PR-body change, or push. Production-code weight\nfor this rereview is zero. Reviewer assessment: R19-4 identified a real but\nuncommon legal state, while the \"by-design\" summary was too strong. The\ntechnical signal is good; the review should keep documented behavior separate\nfrom an approved product decision.\n\n## Product scope decision\n\nThe maintainer chose ordinary native Temporal values or one polyfill copy as\nthe supported group-key inputs. Code that replaces a Temporal prototype or\ncreates a custom prototype that impersonates Temporal is outside this contract.\nSeparately loaded polyfill copies are also outside it. The RED probes above\nremain evidence of those behaviors; they do not establish an in-scope product\nbug under the chosen domain. The active oracle keeps its ordinary own-tag\nplain-object control and does not include a failing altered-prototype case.\n\nThe maintainer also chose to keep alternate representations separate.\n`PT1H` and `PT60M` therefore remain separate groups even though\n`Temporal.Duration.compare()` returns zero. This follows the established\nkind-and-string key rule. The coverage map and oracle opening now state both\ndecisions. No production code or new runtime dependency is needed.\n\nThe updated oracle checks a `PT1H`/`PT60M` pair at the public `groupBy`\ncheckpoint. The 20 Temporal oracle tests and db-ivm TypeScript check pass.\n"}
